# ${project.name}

Maven Reporting Executor turns the report plugins listed in a POM's `<reporting>` section into configured
[`MavenReport`](https://maven.apache.org/shared/maven-reporting-api/apidocs/org/apache/maven/reporting/MavenReport.html)
instances, ready to render. Maven core does not prepare reports itself, so the
[Maven Site Plugin](https://maven.apache.org/plugins/maven-site-plugin/) calls this library during `mvn site`, and
any plugin that renders reports on its own can do the same.

For each report plugin, the executor:

1. resolves the plugin version,
2. selects the report goals to run,
3. merges the report configuration with the plugin's `build/pluginManagement` configuration,
4. loads the plugin in its own class realm and configures each report goal as a mojo,
5. runs the forked lifecycle a report asks for, such as `generate-sources` for `jxr:jxr`.

This page covers what a site user sees, how to call the executor from your own plugin, and what changes in 2.1.0.

${esc.h}${esc.h} Requirements

Version ${project.version} needs Java ${javaVersion} or later. It is built against the Maven ${mavenVersion} core API
and no longer needs `maven-compat`. Version 2.0.1 is the last release built against Maven 3.6.3.

${esc.h}${esc.h} How report plugins are configured during `mvn site`

${esc.h}${esc.h}${esc.h} Plugin version

When a report plugin in `<reporting>` has no `<version>`, the executor takes the first version it finds in:

1. the `build/plugins` section of the POM,
2. the `build/pluginManagement` section of the POM,
3. the plugin repositories, through Maven's `PluginVersionResolver`. This last step logs a warning, because the
   build is no longer reproducible:

```text
[WARNING] Report plugin org.apache.maven.plugins:maven-jxr-plugin has an empty version.
[WARNING]
[WARNING] It is highly recommended to fix these problems because they threaten the stability of your build.
[WARNING]
[WARNING] For this reason, future Maven versions might no longer support building such malformed projects.
[INFO] Configuring report plugin maven-jxr-plugin:3.6.0
```

Declare the version in `<reporting>` or in `build/pluginManagement` to avoid it.

${esc.h}${esc.h}${esc.h} Report goals

If the plugin lists `<reports>` in a `<reportSet>`, only those goals run, and the log says `Configured`. Without a
report list, every goal of the plugin that implements `MavenReport` runs, and the log says `Detected`:

```text
[INFO] Configuring report plugin maven-project-info-reports-plugin:3.9.0
[INFO] Configured 2 reports for maven-project-info-reports-plugin:3.9.0: index, summary
[INFO] Configuring report plugin maven-jxr-plugin:3.6.0
[INFO] Detected 4 reports for maven-jxr-plugin:3.6.0: jxr, jxr-no-fork, test-jxr, test-jxr-no-fork
```

Detected goals that aggregate reports run only in the execution root of a multi-module build with `pom` packaging.
A goal listed explicitly always runs, and a listed goal that is not a report logs a warning and is skipped.

${esc.h}${esc.h}${esc.h} Report configuration

The executor merges configuration in this order, with the first source winning:

1. the `<reportSet>` `<configuration>`,
2. the report plugin's `<configuration>` in `<reporting>`,
3. the plugin's `<configuration>` in `build/pluginManagement`,
4. the mojo's default values.

Parameters that the report goal does not declare are dropped. The `<dependencies>` of the same plugin in
`build/plugins`, or else in `build/pluginManagement`, are added to the report plugin's class path.

${esc.h}${esc.h} Run report plugins from your own plugin

Inject [`MavenReportExecutor`](./apidocs/org/apache/maven/reporting/exec/MavenReportExecutor.html), fill a
[`MavenReportExecutorRequest`](./apidocs/org/apache/maven/reporting/exec/MavenReportExecutorRequest.html), and
render each returned [`MavenReportExecution`](./apidocs/org/apache/maven/reporting/exec/MavenReportExecution.html)
with the context class loader set to the execution's class loader. This is what the Maven Site Plugin does:

```java
@Parameter(defaultValue = "${esc.d}{session}", readonly = true, required = true)
private MavenSession session;

@Parameter(defaultValue = "${esc.d}{project}", readonly = true, required = true)
private MavenProject project;

@Parameter(defaultValue = "${esc.d}{project.reporting.plugins}", readonly = true)
private org.apache.maven.model.ReportPlugin[] reportingPlugins;

@Parameter(defaultValue = "${esc.d}{mojoExecution}", readonly = true, required = true)
private MojoExecution mojoExecution;

private final MavenReportExecutor mavenReportExecutor; // injected through the mojo's @Inject constructor

List<MavenReportExecution> prepareReports() throws MojoExecutionException {
    MavenReportExecutorRequest request = new MavenReportExecutorRequest();
    request.setMavenSession(session);
    request.setExecutionId(mojoExecution.getExecutionId());
    request.setProject(project);
    request.setReportPlugins(reportingPlugins);
    return mavenReportExecutor.buildMavenReports(request);
}

void render(MavenReportExecution execution, Sink sink, SinkFactory sinkFactory, Locale locale)
        throws MavenReportException {
    if (!execution.canGenerateReport()) {
        return;
    }
    ClassLoader original = Thread.currentThread().getContextClassLoader();
    try {
        Thread.currentThread().setContextClassLoader(execution.getClassLoader());
        execution.getMavenReport().generate(sink, sinkFactory, locale);
    } finally {
        Thread.currentThread().setContextClassLoader(original);
    }
}
```

[`canGenerateReport()`](./apidocs/org/apache/maven/reporting/exec/MavenReportExecution.html) already switches the
class loader for you. `generate(Sink, SinkFactory, Locale)` is the Maven Reporting API 4.1.0 entry point; it also
reaches reports that implement only `generate(Sink, Locale)`.

${esc.h}${esc.h}${esc.h} Classes shared with the calling plugin

Each report plugin gets its own class realm, whose parent is the calling plugin's class loader. These packages always
come from the calling plugin, so that the report and the caller agree on the `MavenReport` and `Sink` types:

- `org.apache.maven.reporting`
- `org.apache.maven.doxia.siterenderer`
- `org.apache.maven.doxia.sink`
- `org.apache.maven.doxia.util`

The artifacts `maven-reporting-api`, `doxia-sink-api` and `doxia-site-renderer` are dropped from the report plugin's
own dependencies for the same reason. During `mvn site`, a report therefore runs with the Maven Site Plugin's
Reporting API and Doxia versions, not the ones in its own POM; see
[`DefaultMavenReportExecutor`](https://github.com/apache/maven-reporting-exec/blob/${project.scm.tag}/src/main/java/org/apache/maven/reporting/exec/DefaultMavenReportExecutor.java) for the
exact lists.

${esc.h}${esc.h} Upgrading to 2.1.0

- `MavenReportExecutorRequest.getLocalRepository()` and `setLocalRepository(ArtifactRepository)`, deprecated since
  2.0.0-M10, are removed. Nothing read the value; delete the call.
- The library no longer depends on `maven-compat` and is built against Maven ${mavenVersion} instead of 3.6.3.
- It depends on Maven Reporting API 4.1.0 and Doxia Sink API 2.2.0.

Notice on now obsolete reportPlugin format
------------------------------------------

Before 1.4, the request was filled from a `<reportPlugins>` parameter in the configuration of
`maven-site-plugin` or another plugin that renders reports. That format has been abandoned: it did not support configuration inheritance
([MSITE-484](https://issues.apache.org/jira/browse/MSITE-484)), and Maven core converted `<reporting>` into it only
for the Maven Site Plugin, not for other plugins that render reports. See the
[Maven Site Plugin 3.8.2 documentation](https://maven.apache.org/plugins-archives/maven-site-plugin-3.8.2/maven-3.html#New_Configuration_.28Maven_3_only.2C_no_reports_configuration_inheritance.29)
for the history.

Since 1.4, fill the request from the POM's `<reporting>` section instead, with
`setReportPlugins(org.apache.maven.model.ReportPlugin[])` and the `${esc.d}{project.reporting.plugins}` expression
shown above.
